/* Wealth Lab · Module 11 Income & Investment Taxes · verified source references 2026-11-02 */
window.WealthLabAcademicM11Data={"schema":"wealth-lab-m11-academic-v1","version":"3.0.0","reviewDate":"2026-11-02","taxYear":2026,"lessonIds":["L11.S1","L11.S2","L11.S3","L11.S4"],"lessons":[{"id":"L11.S1","title":"From Income to Tax","subtitle":"Tax law hierarchy, filing records and a reproducible 2026 Form 1040 income-tax computation","prerequisites":["L1.S2 household cash flow and net worth","L6.S1 tax liability, deductions and compliance","L8.S2 the difference between earnings and cash receipts"],"objectives":["Classify wage, business, interest, dividend, gain and excluded receipts under source rules","Distinguish gross pay, W-2 Box 1 wages, adjusted gross income and taxable income","Build an auditable 2026 federal income-tax waterfall including payments and refunds","Trace W-2, 1099-INT, 1099-DIV, 1099-B and Schedule 1 into a return","Compare a deduction, a refundable credit and a nonrefundable credit","Identify simplifications and missing schedules before estimating a real return"],"sections":[{"title":"What the tax system actually measures","body":["Income tax starts from a legal definition of income rather than the cash that happens to land in a bank account. A salary deposit is commonly net of withholding and payroll taxes, but the income-tax return begins with taxable wages shown on Form W-2, Box 1. Borrowed money generally creates both cash and an obligation to repay, not taxable earnings. A transfer between one's own accounts is not new income. A gift, inheritance, insurance benefit, or canceled debt may have different rules; classification is determined by law and facts rather than by how a budgeting app labels the transaction.","The US federal income tax is legislated in the Internal Revenue Code, interpreted through regulations, administrative guidance and court decisions. A taxpayer should distinguish binding statutory rules from a marketing claim or an online chart. IRS publications and instructions are practical primary administrative guides, although the statute and regulations control when there is a conflict. Federal income tax is only one layer: payroll taxes, state income taxes, local property taxes, sales taxes and employer costs use different bases.","An income statement prepared for an employer, a lender and the IRS can display different numbers without contradiction. Gross compensation might be $90,000, taxable wages $84,000 after permitted pre-tax payroll reductions, annual direct deposits $65,000 after withholding and other deductions, and adjusted gross income a still different amount once investment receipts and allowed adjustments are added. Comparing tax liabilities using deposit totals would systematically misstate the return."],"worked":["Gross employer compensation: hypothetical $90,000.","If eligible salary deferrals excluded from W-2 Box 1 are $6,000, Box 1 wages may be $84,000.","A $65,000 net cash deposit is neither the taxable income figure nor the tax owed.","Reconcile the paystub, W-2, bank deposit and benefits documents before starting calculations."],"practice":[["Why is a $5,000 personal loan deposit not generally $5,000 of taxable wages?","The loan produces cash paired with a repayment obligation; it is not compensation or realized enrichment. Cancellation of the debt can create different tax consequences."],["A banking app shows $12,000 transferred from savings to checking as 'income.' What happens on a tax return?","The internal transfer normally creates no new taxable income. Confirm account ownership and source documents rather than importing the app category directly."]]},{"title":"Build a source-document map before calculating","body":["Think of the return as a controlled information system. The employee W-2 reports wages and withheld federal income tax; 1099-INT reports interest; 1099-DIV distinguishes total ordinary dividends from the qualified subset; 1099-B lists securities dispositions and often reported basis; Schedule C reports business revenue less allowable business expenses; Schedule 1 gathers additional income and adjustments. Individual cases may need Schedules 2 and 3, credits and specialty forms. A reconciliation table should state payer, identifying form, tax year, dollar amount, classification, and whether the number is gross or net.","Do not add a qualified dividend amount on top of the total ordinary dividends when Form 1099-DIV shows that qualified dividends are already included in Box 1a. Likewise a broker's Form 1099-B gross proceeds are not income in full: capital gain is realized amount minus adjusted basis. A stock sale for $12,000 with $10,000 adjusted basis creates a $2,000 gain, not $12,000 of salary. Incorrect basis reported by a broker may require Form 8949 adjustments with supporting acquisition records.","Completeness matters more than the number of forms mailed. Taxable interest or side-gig receipts can require reporting even when no 1099 arrives. Keep a year-labeled folder with statements, purchase confirmations, dividend reclassification notices, basis adjustments, and estimated-payment confirmations. Do not place personal identifying numbers in the training app; the laboratory is deliberately anonymous and hypothetical."],"worked":["W-2 Box 1 wages $85,000; federal withholding Box 2 $13,000.","1099-INT taxable interest $1,000.","1099-DIV Box 1a dividends $1,500, of which Box 1b qualified dividends $1,500: include $1,500 in total income once.","1099-B: net long-term gain $4,000 after basis verification; total income $91,500."],"practice":[["Why does adding both 1099-DIV Box 1a and Box 1b double-count income?","Box 1b is the subset of Box 1a eligible for special rate treatment, not a second independent dividend payment."],["What evidence supports a Form 8949 basis correction?","Prior confirmations, transfer statements, reinvestment history, corporate-action records and brokerage basis data with acquisition dates form an audit trail."]]},{"title":"Timing, character and realization","body":["Many individuals use cash-method reporting: taxable income is commonly recognized when actually or constructively received, subject to specific rules. A pay period spanning December and January is typically governed by when the pay becomes available, not simply the days worked. In contrast, a portfolio's unrealized appreciation is usually not taxable solely because its market value rises. A sale or another taxable disposition normally makes a gain or loss realized. Tax law also includes special recognition regimes, such as some distributions and mark-to-market elections, so 'nothing taxed until sold' is not a universal law.","Character is as important as timing. Wage and taxable bank interest commonly use ordinary-income rates. A qualifying dividend or a net long-term capital gain may use preferential rates. Self-employment profit may also generate self-employment tax independent of regular income tax. Municipal bond interest can be excluded from regular federal income tax in qualifying circumstances but may require state-specific treatment and reporting. The return should identify each type before combining totals.","Construct a transaction journal with date acquired, date disposed, proceeds, eligible sale costs, adjusted basis, holding period, and tax character. For multiple lots of one fund, independent verification is essential: a dividend reinvestment can create a new basis lot even though no cash was withdrawn. This discipline is the bridge from Module 8 portfolio accounting to Module 11 taxes."],"worked":["Security purchased for $7,000; year-end value $8,200: unrealized gain $1,200, generally no sale-triggered federal capital gain.","Sell next year for $8,500 with $50 selling expense: amount realized $8,450.","Adjusted basis $7,000 gives realized gain $1,450.","A stock held for one year or less generally produces short-term gain; more than one year generally produces long-term gain."],"practice":[["When can a paper gain become a tax item?","Usually when a sale or other taxable disposition is recognized; timing exceptions exist under particular tax rules, so identify the asset and event."],["Why does a tax worksheet need a holding-period field?","The holding period helps determine whether gain is short-term or long-term and therefore whether ordinary or preferential federal rates may apply."]]},{"title":"Adjusted gross income is not taxable income","body":["The basic federal income-tax waterfall is total income, minus qualifying adjustments to income, equals adjusted gross income (AGI); then subtract allowable deductions to reach taxable income. AGI is a gating figure for many credits and phaseouts. Not every expense paid out of a paycheck is an allowable above-the-line adjustment. A contribution to an employer's traditional 401(k) generally reduces W-2 Box 1 wages before the return; deducting it again as an AGI adjustment would be double counting.","Schedule 1 Part II includes specific allowed adjustments such as certain deductible IRA contributions and HSA contributions where eligibility rules are met; the law imposes caps, timing conditions and phaseouts. A traditional 401(k) salary deferral is different from a Roth 401(k) contribution and different from buying an ETF inside a taxable account. The account label alone does not prove a deduction. A taxpayer must check employment coverage, filing status, income limits and plan documents.","An AGI adjustment can have an indirect value beyond its straightforward marginal rate. Lower AGI may change another income-tested benefit, so the actual incremental tax change can be more complex than multiplying a contribution by a bracket percentage. It cannot be assumed that every pre-tax contribution returns the same cash-tax savings, particularly when credits and phaseouts are present."],"worked":["Total income $92,000; eligible Schedule 1 adjustments $2,000.","AGI = $92,000 − $2,000 = $90,000.","2026 standard deduction for a single non-elderly filer: $16,100.","In an otherwise eligible simple return, taxable income = $90,000 − $16,100 = $73,900."],"practice":[["Why must a 401(k) deferral not be deducted again when Box 1 already excludes it?","It would recognize one economic reduction twice, artificially understating taxable income. Reconcile the wages and retirement contribution reporting first."],["A student knows gross income but not AGI. What is missing?","The legal status and amount of allowable adjustments to income; gross receipts or gross pay alone do not establish the AGI line."]]},{"title":"Deduction, credit, payment and refund are four different concepts","body":["A deduction reduces taxable income. A credit generally reduces the computed tax subject to specific rules. A nonrefundable credit cannot by itself create a tax refund beyond the liability it offsets; a refundable credit can in certain cases create or increase a refund. A payroll withholding payment is not a credit earned by the taxpayer for behavior: it is money already remitted to the government on the taxpayer's behalf. The difference matters because 'getting a refund' does not mean the household paid less tax.","For a simple one-dollar deduction at a 22% federal marginal rate, the first-order reduction in federal ordinary income tax is about $0.22. For an allowable $1,000 credit, the direct liability offset can be $1,000, subject to limits and phaseouts. The deduction can have additional interactions, and tax tables, special capital-gain rates or credits can make an exact return differ; the model must state those features explicitly.","The refund equation is total federal payments and allowable refundable credits minus total federal tax. If positive, a refund is due; if negative, the balance is an amount owed. Withholding changes settlement but not the underlying gross income or the tax computation. Optimizing only for the largest refund can mask lost liquidity during the year."],"worked":["Ordinary taxable income $70,000, approximate marginal rate 22%; eligible $1,000 deduction can save ~$220 before secondary effects.","Eligible $1,000 nonrefundable credit can directly reduce positive tax up to applicable limitations.","Tax after credits $8,000; withholding $9,250 ⇒ $1,250 refund.","Same tax $8,000; withholding $6,750 ⇒ $1,250 due; the underlying tax is identical."],"practice":[["A friend says a tax deduction refunds its full cost. Explain the error.","A deduction subtracts from the tax base, not from tax dollar for dollar. Its tax effect depends on marginal rates and other constraints."],["Does a $4,000 refund prove an efficient filing strategy?","No. It may largely represent excess withholding during the year; review actual total tax, credits, penalties, and cash-flow preferences."]]},{"title":"Complete 2026 single-filer example with qualified dividends","body":["Consider a hypothetical US single filer who is under 65, has no dependents, takes the 2026 standard deduction, and has $85,000 W-2 Box 1 wages, $1,000 taxable interest, $1,500 qualified dividends included within $1,500 ordinary dividends, and $4,000 net long-term gain. There are no Schedule 1 adjustments, capital-loss carryovers, additional taxes or credits. We separate this deliberately simple case from a real Form 1040: special worksheets and the official tax tables can introduce rounding differences.","Total income and AGI are $91,500. Subtract the 2026 single-filer standard deduction of $16,100 to obtain $75,400 taxable income. Of this, $5,500 consists of eligible preferential-rate income, leaving $69,900 taxed at ordinary marginal brackets. The ordinary segment produces $1,240 on the first $12,400, $4,560 on the next $38,000, and $4,290 on the next $19,500 at 22%, totaling $10,090.","In 2026 the single-filer zero-rate capital-gain threshold is $49,450 of taxable income, and the 15% limit reaches $545,500. Because ordinary taxable income already exceeds $49,450, this example places the $5,500 preferred amount in the 15% band: $825. Approximate regular federal tax is therefore $10,915. If withholding is $13,000 and there are no additional taxes, credits or payments, the illustrated refund is $2,085. This ignores tax-table rounding and does not claim to be a filed return."],"worked":["Income: $85,000 + $1,000 + $1,500 + $4,000 = $91,500 AGI.","Taxable = $91,500 − $16,100 = $75,400; preferred portion $5,500, ordinary portion $69,900.","Ordinary tax = $1,240+$4,560+22%×($69,900−$50,400) = $10,090.","Preferred tax = 15%×$5,500 = $825; total ≈ $10,915; refund = $13,000−$10,915 = $2,085."],"practice":[["Where does the 2026 $49,450 capital-gain threshold enter this example?","The preferential income is stacked on top of ordinary taxable income. Because $69,900 already exceeds $49,450, none of the $5,500 qualifies for the zero-rate slice."],["Which missing feature most directly prevents calling this a complete actual return?","Eligibility for credits and additional taxes, complete forms, any carryovers, special rate worksheets, official tax tables and factual documents all must be checked."]]},{"title":"From teaching model to defensible filed return","body":["An academic model is a traceable approximation until it includes every applicable schedule and tax-year instruction. An actual return may require Form 8949 and Schedule D netting, Schedule C and self-employment tax, Schedule 2 additional taxes, Schedule 3 credits and payments, special deductions, phaseouts, capital-gain worksheets, estimated tax, Medicare-related taxes, and state returns. Tax rules can change between a year worked and a return filed in the next calendar year.","Source hierarchy matters. The current year's official forms and instructions answer how to submit, IRS publications explain common concepts, and state revenue agencies govern state adjustments. Recheck final 2026 filing forms when released for 2027 filing. An education app should label every rate by tax year, hold an effective date and verification URL, and never silently reuse one year's threshold in the next. Deductions and bracket amounts from 2025 must not be mixed with 2026 calculations.","Ethically, an adviser must not invent deductions, omit side-gig earnings, disguise taxable proceeds as transfers, or promise that ownership in a trust automatically eliminates tax. These cases can require an enrolled agent, CPA or tax attorney. An analyst's role is to identify the evidence, calculate alternative lawful outcomes, and explain uncertainty—not to guarantee a result."],"worked":["Prepare an audit trail: document → return line → adjustment → computation → withholding/payment.","Check applicable tax year, filing status, age, dependent status, residency and specialized schedules.","Recompute independently, compare to authoritative instructions and record any difference.","Keep personal details out of the educational lab; no account connections or tax preparation are implied."],"practice":[["What does it mean for a tax model to be reproducible?","Another reviewer can trace every input to a dated source, follow the formula and assumptions, and obtain the same results within declared rounding rules."],["Why must a 2026 tax model be updated before returns are filed in 2027?","The IRS may finalize forms and instructions or issue additional guidance; the curriculum should verify applicable year-specific law rather than treating preliminary education as filing advice."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS 2026 revenue procedure: brackets, standard deduction and capital-gain thresholds","https://www.irs.gov/irb/2025-45_IRB"],["IRS 2026 federal standard deduction and tax law overview","https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/working-families-tax-cuts-individuals-and-workers"],["IRS definition of adjusted gross income","https://www.irs.gov/filing/adjusted-gross-income"],["IRS taxable and nontaxable income categories","https://www.irs.gov/filing/taxable-income"],["IRS Publication 550, investments, dividends and capital gain","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"],["IRS Form 1040 official landing page","https://www.irs.gov/forms-pubs/about-form-1040"]],"assessment":{"title":"Tax return reconstruction and source-control memo","prompt":"An unmarried hypothetical 2026 filer age 35 has W-2 Box 1 wages $85,000; W-2 Box 2 withholding $13,000; taxable interest $1,000; ordinary dividends $1,500 of which $1,500 are qualified; and a verified long-term capital gain $4,000. Assume no other income, adjustments, credits, additional taxes or carryovers, and the standard deduction. Prepare total income, AGI, taxable income, ordinary-rate tax, special-rate tax, total federal income tax and refund. Then write 400–600 words explaining source-document verification, tax-year changes and three reasons a real return may differ.","rubric":["Source tracing and classification: 20 points","Correct AGI/deduction/taxable income: 20 points","2026 bracket arithmetic and preferred-income stacking: 30 points","Payments/refund reconciliation: 10 points","Professional limitations, ethical reasoning and sources: 20 points"],"answerKey":["Income/AGI $91,500; standard deduction $16,100; taxable $75,400.","Qualified dividend and net long-term gain $5,500 stacked above $69,900 ordinary taxable income.","Ordinary $10,090; special-rate $825; approximate federal income tax $10,915.","Withholding $13,000 produces approximate $2,085 refund, ignoring official table rounding and omitted items.","Strong memo distinguishes 2026 law from final return forms, documents basis and qualified-dividend treatment, and states assumptions."]}},{"id":"L11.S2","title":"Marginal vs Effective Tax Rate","subtitle":"Progressive schedules, incremental decisions, taxes on different income types and quantitative sensitivity","prerequisites":["L11.S1 taxable-income waterfall and source verification","L7.S3 marginal changes and present value","L10.S3 investment sales and transaction frictions"],"objectives":["Derive a progressive income-tax function from tax brackets","Compute statutory, marginal, average and effective tax rates without conflating their denominators","Model a $1 income change and quantify a $1 deduction or credit","Analyze ordinary-income and preferential-capital-gain stacking","Explain phaseouts and why a practical effective marginal rate may exceed a bracket","Evaluate Roth/traditional or extra-work decisions without assuming immediate savings are the whole answer"],"sections":[{"title":"A bracket is a price on the next slice, not on every dollar","body":["A progressive rate schedule applies different rates to successive portions of taxable income. A single filer entering the 22% federal bracket does not suddenly owe 22% on their first dollar. The marginal statutory bracket is the slope of the federal ordinary-income tax schedule over an interval, not the average share paid on all wages. This distinction underlies rational choices about overtime, charitable deductions, tax-deferred retirement contributions and investment sales.","For 2026, a single filer has ordinary-income thresholds $12,400 at 10%, from $12,400 to $50,400 at 12%, and from $50,400 to $105,700 at 22%, before the next bracket. At $70,000 ordinary taxable income, tax on the first $12,400 is $1,240, on the next $38,000 is $4,560, and on the next $19,600 is $4,312. The resulting $10,112 is about 14.45% of taxable income, even though the next ordinary dollar is in the 22% band.","A bracket table must be attached to the correct filing status and tax year. The federal standard deduction creates another transformation before brackets apply. An analyst who applies bracket percentages to gross wages instead of taxable income is answering the wrong question. Additional credit phaseouts and payroll taxes mean the total tax cost of earning a dollar may differ from the basic federal marginal rate."],"worked":["2026 single taxable ordinary income $70,000.","$12,400×10%=$1,240; ($50,400−$12,400)×12%=$4,560.","($70,000−$50,400)×22%=$4,312.","Total ordinary tax $10,112; average over taxable =14.4457%; marginal statutory ordinary rate =22%."],"practice":[["Why does crossing the $50,400 breakpoint not reprice the first $50,400?","Progressive tax is piecewise: the new rate only applies to the portion in that higher bracket. Earlier brackets retain their lower rates."],["A $70,000 taxpayer claims an effective rate of 22% because of the bracket. What is wrong?","22% is the current ordinary marginal band; the modeled tax divided by ordinary taxable income is about 14.45%, and divided by AGI or wages differs again."]]},{"title":"Deriving a piecewise function and validating continuity","body":["Write the tax function T(x) as the sum of each bracket's width multiplied by its rate, up to taxable ordinary income x. For a single 2026 filer with x between $50,400 and $105,700, the prior brackets total $5,800 and T(x) = $5,800 + 0.22×(x−$50,400). A formula that starts with 22%×x violates the structure. Tax schedules are continuous at a bracket boundary: exactly at $50,400 both the second and third segment formulations must give $5,800.","The derivative of T with respect to taxable ordinary income, away from thresholds and special rules, is the applicable statutory marginal tax rate. However, household disposable income changes with FICA, credit restrictions, state taxes and sometimes benefit eligibility. An actual effective marginal tax rate can also be influenced by the rate at which an income-tested credit disappears. Those interactions must be modeled separately, never implied by a simple bracket table.","Use a unit test with x exactly at the threshold, one dollar below and one dollar above. The tax difference at the boundary should be the lower rate on the dollar below and the higher rate on the dollar above. This is an example of software validation that teaches the economics: if a calculator shows a thousand-dollar tax jump for a one-dollar raise at a normal federal bracket boundary, its implementation is wrong."],"worked":["2026 T(50,400) = $1,240 + $4,560 = $5,800.","T(50,401) = $5,800 +0.22×$1 = $5,800.22.","T(50,399) = $1,240 +0.12×($50,399−$12,400)=$5,799.88.","The function is continuous: no sudden change of tax on earlier income."],"practice":[["What does the derivative T'(x) measure when the bracket is stable?","The approximate additional federal ordinary income tax from one more dollar of ordinary taxable income before phaseouts or special regimes."],["Why should tax software test exactly at bracket thresholds?","Boundary tests reveal off-by-one errors, misapplied whole-income rates and discontinuities that can otherwise create materially misleading decisions."]]},{"title":"Average burden depends on the denominator","body":["Statutory marginal rate, average rate on taxable income, and effective rate on adjusted gross income answer different questions. Suppose a single filer has $90,000 AGI and takes the $16,100 2026 standard deduction, leaving $73,900 ordinary taxable income. The basic federal ordinary tax is $5,800 +22%×$23,500=$10,970. Dividing by taxable income gives roughly 14.84%; dividing by AGI gives about 12.19%. Neither is the percentage of cash that reaches the checking account.","Many public tax comparisons use tax divided by an income measure, but the precise denominator varies: wages, total income, AGI, taxable income, or economic income including untaxed appreciation. Household FICA and employer payroll tax may be in or out of the tax total. The analyst should always label both numerator and denominator. Without that declaration, two accurate-looking tax statistics can refer to materially different burdens.","Refine the estimate by disaggregating regular federal income tax, federal self-employment tax, state taxes, and other mandatory liabilities. An employee's paycheck withholding may exceed or fall short of actual year-end tax, so paystub percentages can be particularly misleading."],"worked":["AGI $90,000; 2026 single standard deduction $16,100; ordinary taxable $73,900.","Tax = $5,800 +22%×($73,900−$50,400) = $10,970.","Average on taxable = $10,970/$73,900 ≈14.84%.","Effective federal income tax/AGI = $10,970/$90,000 ≈12.19%; excludes payroll and state tax."],"practice":[["Can two tax rate percentages be different and both be mathematically correct?","Yes, if one divides by taxable income and another by AGI or wages; every rate must identify its numerator and denominator."],["Why is income-tax withholding not an appropriate stand-in for annual liability?","Withholding is a payment mechanism that may generate a refund or amount due, not the statutory computation of annual tax."]]},{"title":"The value of an incremental deduction or credit","body":["Financial planning is about comparing the after-tax change from a specific decision. If an eligible $2,000 traditional retirement contribution is deductible at the 22% federal ordinary marginal rate and no other restrictions intervene, the modeled current federal tax reduction is about $440. That does not make the contribution free: the household still directs $2,000 of cash into a restricted retirement investment, with future withdrawal taxes and investment outcomes to evaluate.","A $2,000 nonrefundable credit can reduce tax by up to $2,000 where the taxpayer qualifies and has sufficient liability. It is not identical to a $2,000 deduction. Some tax credits are refundable; others are not; phaseouts and income limits change the outcome. Treat credit eligibility as a legal question, not a calculator input assumed true by default.","An opportunity-cost model compares present value of tax saved today, future ordinary tax on withdrawals, tax-free Roth distributions if qualified, current liquidity, employer match, and years until the money is needed. A taxpayer purchasing a house within twelve months can have a high marginal rate but low capacity to lock away money. A tax-optimized portfolio is not necessarily a life-optimized portfolio."],"worked":["Eligible $2,000 reduction in ordinary taxable income at stable 22% ⇒ ~$440 immediate federal tax reduction.","A permitted $2,000 nonrefundable credit with adequate pre-credit liability ⇒ $2,000 direct reduction.","If a credit is income-phased-out, use the eligibility formula before applying dollar-for-dollar logic.","Tax benefit is a cash-flow component, not the investment return or a refund guarantee."],"practice":[["What is the immediate tax difference between a deduction and a credit?","A deduction lowers the taxable base; a qualified credit offsets tax, subject to refundable or nonrefundable limitations and eligibility restrictions."],["Why can a pretax retirement contribution be inappropriate for a near-term homebuyer even when it lowers tax?","It restricts current liquidity, can incur withdrawal consequences, and may conflict with cash reserves and purchase timing; tax savings alone do not resolve the choice."]]},{"title":"Married status, income splitting and the danger of false shortcuts","body":["Filing status is not an optional mathematical setting chosen merely to minimize tax. Marriage, dependency and household facts determine lawful options. A married couple may compare married filing jointly and married filing separately when allowed, but credits, deductions, capital-loss limits and liability considerations can make the result non-obvious. Some households see a marriage benefit, others a penalty, depending on income distribution and tax provisions.","For 2026 married filing jointly, the ordinary brackets begin 10% up to $24,800, 12% up to $100,800, and 22% through $211,400; the standard deduction is $32,200. A simple hypothetical couple with $92,000 combined ordinary income and no adjustments has $59,800 taxable income. Tax is $2,480 +12%×($59,800−$24,800)=$6,680. That is approximately 7.26% of gross income, while the next ordinary taxable dollar faces a 12% federal marginal bracket.","Do not infer from these few numbers that marriage always creates a discount. Special tax regimes, joint-liability rules, deductions, student-loan calculations, dependents and state law may matter. The model is a deliberately bounded instructional case."],"worked":["2026 MFJ income and AGI $92,000, no preferred-income items.","Taxable income $92,000−$32,200=$59,800.","Tax =10%×$24,800 +12%×$35,000 = $6,680.","Effective federal regular income tax over AGI ≈ 7.26%; marginal statutory ordinary rate 12%."],"practice":[["Why must an academic married-filing comparison identify the legal filing status?","The taxpayer's actual status and qualifying conditions determine which schedule applies; choosing the lowest percentage without establishing eligibility is not compliance."],["Does the 12% bracket mean this couple pays 12% of all $92,000?","No. The first taxable slice uses 10%, the later slice 12%, and the standard deduction reduces the base before any bracket rate is applied."]]},{"title":"Qualified gains have a separate tax schedule layered on top","body":["Preferential capital-gain and qualified-dividend rates require a second tax curve, not a single universal marginal rate. For most eligible net long-term gain, the 2026 zero-rate threshold is $49,450 of taxable income for single filers and $98,900 for joint filers. The applicable 15% bands then extend to $545,500 and $613,700 respectively. These amounts are based on taxable income, including the ordinary income below the preferential slice, and certain gains have distinct special rates.","Take a single filer with $46,000 ordinary taxable income and $7,000 of eligible long-term gain, with no special gain categories. Total taxable income is $53,000. The first $3,450 of preferential income fills the remaining room below $49,450 and is taxed at 0%; the remaining $3,550 is taxed at 15%, yielding $532.50 of incremental tax on that gain. It is incorrect to charge zero on the whole gain simply because the taxpayer's ordinary income was below $49,450.","Unearned income or certain high-income situations may trigger the net investment income tax (NIIT) at 3.8% on the smaller of net investment income and modified AGI above its applicable threshold. The ordinary short-term gain, qualified dividend, and NIIT computations must not be conflated. The lab will isolate the main stacking effect; a complete return may have other tax worksheets."],"worked":["Ordinary taxable base $46,000; preferential gain $7,000; combined taxable $53,000.","2026 single 0% threshold $49,450; zero-rated slice = $49,450−$46,000 = $3,450.","15%-rated slice $7,000−$3,450=$3,550.","Simplified capital-gain tax = 0%×$3,450+15%×$3,550=$532.50."],"practice":[["Does the full $7,000 qualify for zero percent simply because ordinary taxable income is $46,000?","No. Stacking places the $7,000 on top of $46,000, leaving only $3,450 of zero-rate space under the $49,450 combined taxable threshold."],["What is a key difference between NIIT and the regular long-term capital-gain rate?","NIIT is a separate 3.8% tax triggered by specific modified-AGI and net-investment-income rules, not a replacement for the basic 0/15/20% calculation."]]},{"title":"Effective marginal rates can include phaseouts and clawbacks","body":["A household's true incremental tax burden is the change in all relevant taxes and benefits when a decision changes income. A statutory 22% bracket does not cap the effective marginal rate. An income-tested credit that falls by $100 when additional income rises by $1,000 adds an effective 10 percentage-point cost to that specific interval, making the modeled combined change about 32% before state tax or payroll taxes. This is a mathematical example, not a claim about any particular credit.","Phaseout rules have thresholds, filing-status qualifications, earned-income tests and sometimes discontinuous eligibility conditions. In some circumstances an extra dollar can affect a credit, deduction limit or premium calculation more than a bracket alone predicts. To evaluate extra work hours or a conversion from traditional to Roth retirement accounts, compare the tax outcome with and without the change using a complete set of applicable rules.","Tax planning should avoid perverse incentives created by focusing on marginal numbers alone. Higher gross pay typically leaves a household better off across normal federal tax brackets, even if the next dollar is taxed more heavily than earlier ones. Real situations with benefit cliffs can be exceptions requiring complete facts. Investigate the full cash-flow difference rather than refusing all extra income due to a misunderstood 'higher bracket.'"],"worked":["Baseline income increase +$1,000 at 22% ordinary statutory rate ⇒+$220 regular income tax.","Hypothetical income-tested credit decreases $100 ⇒ combined net cost $320.","Illustrative effective marginal impact $320/$1,000=32%, excluding all other taxes and benefits.","Document which rule causes each change; an invented phaseout should never appear as actual tax law."],"practice":[["Why can a real effective marginal rate exceed the statutory tax bracket?","Credit and deduction phaseouts, state taxes, payroll taxes or benefit adjustments can change with additional income, adding marginal costs beyond the federal rate schedule."],["Should a person reject overtime merely because they moved from a 12% to a 22% bracket?","No. The higher bracket generally applies only to later taxable dollars. Compare the additional net cash after all applicable taxes and benefit effects."]]},{"title":"Sensitivity analysis and deciding what to verify","body":["An analyst should test a tax model over changes in filing status, preferred-income amount, contributions and time. A smooth graph of the tax function will change slope at statutory brackets; preferential gains can change slope at their own thresholds; credit phaseouts can add further bends. This means that a claim such as 'every dollar deferred now saves exactly 22 cents' should be presented as locally conditional, not universally true.","Tax consequences also span time. Compare tax saved today to expected future taxes, investment returns, liquidity needs and uncertainty in future legislation. The present value of a deferred tax bill depends on withdrawal date, asset growth and applicable future rates. A student must be able to construct both an optimistic and a conservative scenario and state why neither is guaranteed.","A professional-grade memorandum records the relevant IRS schedule, tax year, assumptions, tax-rate denominator, omitted items and a recommendation conditional on those facts. It distinguishes an educational mathematical approximation from advice specific to a taxpayer's complete return."],"worked":["Scenario A: ordinary taxable income $70,000 ⇒ basic federal ordinary tax $10,112.","Scenario B: $72,000 ⇒ tax $10,552; incremental tax $440 on $2,000.","Scenario C: a qualified $2,000 nonrefundable credit with adequate liability ⇒ up to $2,000 tax reduction.","Carry alternative scenarios forward to liquid cash, retirement restrictions and future withdrawal rates."],"practice":[["What should be displayed next to a 'tax savings' figure in an app?","Tax year, filing status, bracket method, type of income, credits and phaseouts considered, and whether the figure is incremental or total tax."],["Why is future retirement taxation relevant to choosing a current deduction?","Traditional deferral commonly shifts taxation to future distributions; comparing only today's savings hides the future tax and liquidity trade-offs."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS 2026 federal rate tables and preferential thresholds","https://www.irs.gov/irb/2025-45_IRB"],["IRS 2026 standard deductions and worker tax law","https://www.irs.gov/newsroom/working-families-tax-cuts-individuals-and-workers"],["IRS adjusted gross income definition","https://www.irs.gov/filing/adjusted-gross-income"],["IRS capital gains and losses topic","https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc409"],["IRS net investment income tax thresholds","https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc559"],["IRS withholding estimator","https://www.irs.gov/individuals/tax-withholding-estimator"]],"assessment":{"title":"Progressive-rate audit and behavior analysis","prompt":"Derive and graph the first three brackets of the 2026 single-filer ordinary tax function. Compute liability at $50,399, $50,400, $50,401 and $70,000. Explain continuity and calculate the $70,000 marginal and average rates. Then evaluate an extra $2,000 of wages, a qualifying $2,000 deduction and a $2,000 nonrefundable credit. Independently compute a single filer's eligible capital gain when ordinary taxable income is $46,000 and long-term gains are $7,000. Write a one-page memo about how a cash-constrained first-time homebuyer should compare current tax savings with liquidity.","rubric":["Piecewise function and continuity tests: 25 points","Marginal/average/effective labels with denominators: 20 points","Deduction versus credit quantitative comparison: 20 points","Capital-gain stacking: 20 points","Interpretation of liquidity, incentives and sources: 15 points"],"answerKey":["T(50,399)=$5,799.88; T(50,400)=$5,800; T(50,401)=$5,800.22.","T(70,000)=$10,112; marginal 22%; average over taxable income ≈14.4457%.","+$2,000 ordinary wages =>+$440 federal regular tax in this interval. Qualifying $2,000 deduction => ~$440 less. Eligible $2,000 credit => up to $2,000 less.","Of $7,000 long-term gain, $3,450 is in 0% space and $3,550 taxed at 15% =>$532.50 incremental regular tax.","Quality memo does not treat bracket rate as the share of all wages and considers liquidity and future tax rates."]}},{"id":"L11.S3","title":"Capital Gains, Basis & Dividends","subtitle":"Tax-lot accounting, disposition character, preferential-rate stacking and investment reporting","prerequisites":["L8.S1 stock ownership and corporate actions","L9.S3 ETF and fund mechanics","L11.S1 taxable-income reconciliation","L11.S2 bracket and preferential-rate mathematics"],"objectives":["Build a complete adjusted-cost-basis ledger with purchases, reinvestments and dispositions","Distinguish amount realized, realized gain, recognized taxable gain and unrealized appreciation","Determine short/long holding categories and net short/long results","Read 1099-DIV and 1099-B without double counting or blind trust in reported basis","Compute simplified 2026 qualified-dividend and long-term-gain tax using the stacking rule","Diagnose NIIT exposure, retirement-account location and inheritance basis exceptions"],"sections":[{"title":"The economic investment return is not the taxable gain","body":["An investment earns economic return from price changes and income. The tax return normally measures realized and recognized events rather than all changes in quoted market value. Price appreciation in an unsold stock usually has no current capital-gain tax, yet a cash dividend paid by the company can be taxable even if immediately reinvested. An investment can thus have an excellent one-year economic return and little realized capital gain, or negative total return while producing taxable distributions.","For a taxable sale, realized gain or loss generally equals amount realized minus adjusted basis. Amount realized commonly starts with sales proceeds, reduced by eligible selling costs where applicable. Adjusted basis generally begins with what was paid, including appropriate acquisition costs, and can change through corporate actions, return-of-capital distributions and prior wash-sale adjustments. Recognition determines how much of the realized amount is actually included under tax rules. Realized and recognized are not interchangeable when special exclusions or deferrals exist.","An investment accountant reconciles trade confirmation, account statement and tax forms, with dates, lot IDs, quantity, fees and basis adjustments. A market value graph cannot substitute for transaction records. Tax-aware investing starts with consistent records, not with hunting for a clever deduction at year-end."],"worked":["Purchase shares for $10,000 and sell for gross $13,000 with $100 eligible selling costs.","Amount realized $12,900; original adjusted basis $10,000; realized gain $2,900.","If fully taxable and recognized, the gain enters the applicable short- or long-term netting calculation.","Unrealized price increase before sale is tracked for planning but is not normally current realized capital gain."],"practice":[["Does a $3,000 increase in an unsold stock automatically create $3,000 current taxable capital gain?","No, normal tax recognition generally follows sale or another disposition; holding-market appreciation by itself is ordinarily unrealized."],["Why can return-of-capital distributions raise the gain on a later sale?","They can reduce the investment's basis, subject to limits and specific rules, increasing later realized gain for the same disposal proceeds."]]},{"title":"Build an adjusted-basis ledger with dividend reinvestment","body":["Cost basis is a history, not merely a broker's current number. Imagine 100 shares bought at $50 each, then $300 of taxable dividends automatically reinvested into six new shares at $50 each. The economic account value has increased by the dividends and subsequent market changes, but the reinvestment adds a new $300 acquisition lot. Total basis is $5,300 across 106 shares. Omitting the reinvestment would overstate future taxable gain by $300.","Stock splits reallocate aggregate basis over a new share count rather than generally creating a gain on the split alone. A 2-for-1 split of 100 shares with $5,000 aggregate basis creates 200 shares with $25 per-share basis if all holdings are identical for that lot. Spin-offs can allocate basis according to prescribed relative market values. Employee equity compensation can require additional reconciliation to avoid taxing wages and later sale gains inconsistently. These specialized issues deserve issuer notices and professional verification.","Basis rules are not identical for every asset. Real estate adjusts for certain closing costs, capital improvements and depreciation. Digital assets have their own tracing and reporting complexities. Mutual funds may permit an average-basis method under conditions, but individual stock sales commonly rely on tax-lot identification rules. A defensible ledger lists the authority for each adjustment."],"worked":["Lot A 100 shares at $50 ⇒ basis $5,000.","Reinvested dividend $300 acquires 6 shares at $50 ⇒ Lot B basis $300.","Combined basis $5,300 on 106 shares; do not subtract dividends from taxable income because reinvested.","If all 106 sold for $6,360 and no selling costs, total gain $1,060."],"practice":[["Why do reinvested taxable dividends count as basis in newly purchased shares?","The dividend can be taxable when paid, while the reinvestment is an additional purchase that acquires an asset with its own cost basis. Omitting basis could tax the same value again at sale."],["What happens to a lot's aggregate basis in a simple 2-for-1 stock split?","Normally aggregate basis is spread across twice as many shares, making per-share basis half the prior figure, absent special corporate-action rules."]]},{"title":"Tax-lot selection is part of portfolio implementation","body":["An investor who bought 100 shares at $40 and 100 at $70 has two $4,000 and $7,000 original basis lots. If 100 shares are sold at $80, realized gain is $4,000 from the low-basis lot or $1,000 from the high-basis lot. Which lot is treated as sold depends on permitted identification procedures and actual broker execution. A retrospective spreadsheet selection that was not properly designated at the time of sale cannot magically change the tax lot.","Specific identification generally requires identifying the particular shares in the manner and time allowed and receiving broker confirmation. If adequate identification is absent, first-in, first-out can apply. Average-basis elections are available for certain eligible mutual-fund shares and dividend-reinvestment plans, with rules and consequences. Confirm the method with the custodian and account type before trading; an investor may be unable to reverse an election casually.","A lower gain today is not always the best long-term strategy. Selling high-basis shares can conserve low-basis lots for a later low-rate year or leave larger potential gains for heirs if an applicable date-of-death basis adjustment is available. But that future treatment is uncertain and differs for lifetime gifts, jointly owned interests and estate situations. The strategy must consider diversification, opportunity cost and estate objectives, not just immediate tax."],"worked":["100 shares at $40 = Lot A basis $4,000; 100 shares at $70 = Lot B basis $7,000.","Sell 100 shares for $8,000 gross proceeds.","Lot A recognized gain could be $4,000; Lot B gain $1,000, assuming valid specific identification.","At hypothetical 15% long-term tax rate, isolated current difference is $450; future consequences are not modeled."],"practice":[["Can a taxpayer choose an arbitrary high-basis lot when filing months after a sale?","No. Tax-lot identification must satisfy applicable contemporaneous rules and broker records; a later preference alone is insufficient."],["What non-tax risk can arise from refusing to sell a low-basis position?","Avoiding realized tax may leave an investor overconcentrated in a risky security and expose the household to losses exceeding the modeled tax benefit."]]},{"title":"Holding period and capital-loss netting","body":["Most capital assets sold after more than one year are long-term; those held one year or less are short-term. Certain rules alter holding periods, including some corporate actions and wash-sale replacement shares. Short-term net gains are generally taxed at ordinary income rates while qualifying net long-term gains may use preferential rates. Special asset categories, such as collectibles and some real-property depreciation gains, can have separate maximum rates; a generic 15% calculation does not apply to every sale.","Capital gains and losses are aggregated with short-term and long-term subtotals on Schedule D. A net short-term loss can offset net long-term gain under the netting rules, and vice versa. If overall capital loss exceeds gains, individuals can generally deduct up to $3,000 per year against other income, or $1,500 if married filing separately, with eligible remaining losses carried forward subject to applicable rules. The deduction limitation is not a permanent forgiveness of every loss.","Practical timing matters: a sale dated December 31 can be in one tax year while a January sale belongs to the next. But attempts to manipulate prices or invent sales are not tax planning. Trade and settlement records, account type and the IRS reporting conventions control. Portfolio models should distinguish a theoretical unrealized loss from a legitimate, completed disposition."],"worked":["Tax year net short-term capital loss −$2,000 and net long-term capital gain +$4,000.","After required cross-category netting, simplified net capital gain +$2,000 remains.","Another example: net loss −$8,000 with no other capital gains, single filer.","Up to $3,000 generally offsets ordinary income in that year; $5,000 potentially carries forward, subject to actual return rules."],"practice":[["Can the full $8,000 net capital loss always offset wages in one year?","No. For individuals, the usual ordinary-income capital-loss deduction limit is $3,000 per year ($1,500 married filing separately); eligible remaining losses carry forward."],["Why do we keep separate short- and long-term schedules until netting is finished?","They initially have different tax character and the prescribed netting process determines what preferential gain remains or how losses carry forward."]]},{"title":"Capital gains are stacked above ordinary taxable income","body":["The 2026 federal maximum zero and 15% thresholds for eligible net long-term capital gain and qualified dividends depend on taxable income, not on the gain amount alone. For an unmarried filer, the zero rate stops at $49,450 taxable income and the 15% tier stops at $545,500. For married filing jointly, the respective thresholds are $98,900 and $613,700. The regular 0/15/20% rates are not universal to every gain, and the separate 3.8% NIIT can add to total burden for certain taxpayers.","The computational sequence is to identify ordinary taxable income after deductions, add eligible preferential income, and fill zero-rate space first, then 15% and if necessary 20%. An investor with $46,000 ordinary taxable income and $7,000 eligible long-term gains has only $3,450 of 0% space left, so $3,550 is in the 15% band and tax on the gains is about $532.50. A person who looks at a headline 0% rate without stacking would wrongly assume all gains are untaxed.","The filing return uses a Qualified Dividends and Capital Gain Tax Worksheet or Schedule D Tax Worksheet as applicable. It is an error to simply multiply the entire taxable income by the capital-gain rate. A professional model should isolate non-preferred categories, netting, and any additional investment taxes before reporting the actual liability."],"worked":["Single ordinary taxable base $46,000; eligible net long-term gain $7,000; total taxable $53,000.","Zero-rated portion max(0, $49,450−$46,000) = $3,450.","Remaining $3,550×15% = $532.50 simplified tax on gain.","No assumed NIIT, special gain category, tax-table effects or credit phaseout."],"practice":[["How does ordinary income affect 0% capital-gain capacity?","Ordinary taxable income occupies the lower portion of the taxable-income scale, reducing or eliminating the space available for preferential gains at zero percent."],["Why might the statutory '0% capital gains' headline mislead a retiree?","Other taxable income can fill the zero-rate range; state taxes, NIIT and other taxable events may still apply. Full return context matters."]]},{"title":"Dividends: cash yield, ordinary classification and the qualified subset","body":["A dividend is a distribution from a corporation or fund, but tax character is determined by the payer's reporting and eligibility rules, not by the word 'dividend' on a trading screen. Form 1099-DIV Box 1a generally reports ordinary dividends. Box 1b identifies those within Box 1a that are qualified dividends, potentially eligible for preferential rates. Capital-gain distributions can be reported separately, and nondividend distributions may change basis. A fund's advertised yield is not necessarily its after-tax investor return.","For many common stock dividends, preferential eligibility normally requires the shareholder to hold the stock more than 60 days within the 121-day window beginning 60 days before the ex-dividend date; other eligibility and hedge/related-payment rules may apply. Some preferred shares have different holding-period requirements. Merely holding a share on the record date is not enough to prove qualified treatment. Dividend reinvestment does not by itself erase the year's dividend tax liability in a taxable account.","Compare two fictional portfolios both receiving $1,000 of dividends: one entirely nonqualified and subject to 22% ordinary marginal tax, one entirely qualified at an applicable 15% rate. With no secondary effects the modeled current federal tax is $220 versus $150. This $70 difference is economically meaningful but not sufficient to select a fund: price risk, diversification, total return, expense ratio, state taxes and account location also matter."],"worked":["1099-DIV Box 1a = $1,000 ordinary dividends; Box 1b = $700 qualified.","Total dividend income $1,000, not $1,700.","If ordinary unqualified $300 is taxed at 22% and qualified $700 at 15%, simplified tax = $66+$105=$171.","Cash dividend reinvestment can increase acquisition cost basis while still generating taxable dividends."],"practice":[["Why is Box 1b not added on top of Box 1a?","Qualified dividends are included within total ordinary dividends and are singled out only for preferential tax-rate computations."],["Can a dividend be unqualified even when it comes from a large familiar company?","Yes. Eligibility depends on holding-period and other conditions in addition to the issuer's character."]]},{"title":"NIIT, special assets and account location","body":["The net investment income tax is a separate 3.8% federal levy on the smaller of defined net investment income or modified AGI exceeding a filing-status threshold. As of the current guidance, thresholds include $200,000 single or head of household, $250,000 married filing jointly and $125,000 married filing separately. It cannot be approximated just by applying 3.8% to every investment account distribution. Retirement distributions have their own treatment and can affect modified AGI even when excluded from the definition of net investment income.","Taxable brokerage accounts, traditional tax-deferred accounts and Roth accounts can create very different current tax reporting for the same underlying ETF. A dividend inside an IRA generally does not produce the same immediate 1099-DIV liability as a dividend in a regular taxable brokerage account; eventual distributions and eligibility rules govern later taxation. Asset location is not the same as asset allocation. A highly tax-efficient holding in a taxable account may outperform moving it solely to avoid current income when withdrawal restrictions and future tax are included.","Inheritance and gifting introduce another dimension. Property inherited from a decedent generally receives a tax basis determined under date-of-death valuation rules, subject to statutory exceptions; lifetime gifts can involve carryover and dual-basis rules. A revocable trust does not automatically generate a new basis merely because property is retitled. Review ownership, inclusion in the estate, asset type, beneficiary facts and current tax law with a qualified professional."],"worked":["Single taxpayer MAGI $212,000; defined net investment income $8,000.","MAGI excess over $200,000 NIIT threshold is $12,000.","NIIT base = min($8,000,$12,000)=$8,000 ⇒ NIIT $304 at 3.8%.","Tax-deferred/Roth account consequences require distinct retirement-account rules."],"practice":[["If MAGI exceeds a NIIT threshold by $5,000 and net investment income is $20,000, what is the basic NIIT base?","The lower amount is $5,000, so the simple 3.8% computation is $190; detailed rules can change the definition of MAGI and net investment income."],["Does placing appreciated stock in a revocable trust automatically reset its tax basis immediately?","No. Titling alone does not create a basis step-up; inheritance, estate inclusion and other statutory conditions control when a basis adjustment is available."]]},{"title":"Prepare a tax-lot investment memorandum","body":["The final analytical task combines actual brokerage workflows and tax theory. Construct a lot-level schedule with acquisition date, quantity, acquisition price, reinvested dividends, adjusted basis, disposition date, amount realized, gain or loss, recognized amount, short/long category and the source document backing every field. Independently reconcile aggregated sales to Forms 1099-B and 8949 and the resulting Schedule D. Isolate capital-gain distributions and qualified dividends from 1099-DIV without duplication.","Then compare two defensible portfolio actions: sell an overweight low-basis holding now, or use contributions and alternative lots to reduce concentration over time. Quantify estimated federal and state taxes and what the family gains in diversification and access to cash. Delaying a tax bill can have present value, but concentrating every dollar to avoid a 15% gain tax can put a house fund at unacceptable market risk.","State what the model omits: relevant tax-year capital-gain thresholds, different character of certain gains, NIIT, timing, broker lot identification, residency and estate uncertainties. Show a reviewer exactly which assumptions would reverse your recommendation. This is applied investment accounting, not a universal instruction to trade or defer taxation."],"worked":["Create a worksheet with at least three tax lots and one reinvested dividend.","Reconcile 1099-B sales proceeds less basis to lot-by-lot gain.","Compute gain under a valid high-basis sale and a low-basis sale, reporting the difference.","Write a risk memo on diversification and household liquidity before endorsing a tax-driven trade."],"practice":[["What makes a basis ledger suitable for third-party review?","Each material field ties to a dated broker record, corporate action or verified adjustment, with a consistent lot-selection and holding-period method."],["When can paying capital-gain tax now be a sound decision?","When de-risking, diversification, needed liquidity or future tax uncertainty outweighs the present value of deferring tax on an overly concentrated position."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS Publication 550—investment income, adjusted basis, dividends and holding period","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"],["IRS Schedule D instructions—netting and wash sales","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i1040sd"],["IRS Form 8949 instructions","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8949"],["IRS 2026 rates and capital-gain ceilings","https://www.irs.gov/irb/2025-45_IRB"],["IRS Net Investment Income Tax topic","https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc559"],["IRS Publication 551—Basis of Assets","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p551"],["IRS Publication 559—survivors, executors and administrators","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p559"]],"assessment":{"title":"Brokerage lot reconciliation and investment-tax committee","prompt":"Construct a three-lot brokerage tax ledger: buy 100 shares at $40, 100 at $70 and acquire six additional shares by reinvesting a $300 dividend at $50. Assume all purchases held more than one year. Sell 100 shares for $8,000 with properly documented specific identification of Lot A versus Lot B; compare federal capital-gain effect at an assumed 15% applicable rate. Separately reconcile a Form 1099-DIV reporting $1,000 Box 1a dividends, $700 Box 1b qualified dividends. Then analyze a single filer with $46,000 ordinary taxable income and $7,000 eligible long-term gain in 2026, and write a one-page defense of trading now versus waiting based on concentration and a home down-payment obligation.","rubric":["Acquisition and reinvestment ledger accuracy: 20 points","Lot selection, gain and holding-period analysis: 20 points","Dividend reporting without duplication: 15 points","2026 capital-gain stacking and independent recomputation: 25 points","Liquidity, diversification, NIIT and ethical caveats: 20 points"],"answerKey":["Lots A/B/reinvestment basis $4,000/$7,000/$300; aggregate basis $11,300.","100-share disposal at $8,000 yields $4,000 gain on A, $1,000 on B; at assumed 15% isolated taxes $600 and $150 (difference $450).","Box 1a $1,000 is total ordinary dividend, Box 1b $700 subset; unqualified remainder $300.","2026 stack $46,000 ordinary plus $7,000 gain: $3,450 zero rate, $3,550 at 15% ⇒ $532.50.","Valid conclusions consider actual lot identification, state tax, concentration, fees and future rates."]}},{"id":"L11.S4","title":"Tax-Loss Harvesting and State Taxes","subtitle":"Legal loss recognition, wash-sale basis, federal–Connecticut differences and after-tax decision models","prerequisites":["L11.S1 return construction","L11.S2 marginal-rate and preferential-rate mechanics","L11.S3 cost basis, lots, holding periods and Schedule D","L10.S4 investment policy and liquidity limits"],"objectives":["Apply short-term and long-term loss netting and carryforward limitations","Analyze the 61-day wash-sale window and adjusted replacement basis","Recognize IRA replacement purchases and cross-account compliance limitations","Build a conceptual federal-to-Connecticut adjusted-gross-income bridge","Compare harvesting benefits after current tax, eventual tax, transaction costs and tracking risk","Create a sourced, auditable investment-tax policy with tax-year versioning"],"sections":[{"title":"Harvesting is a strategy to realize an actual loss, not a tax refund machine","body":["Tax-loss harvesting deliberately sells a declining taxable investment, generally to recognize a capital loss that may offset realized gains or limited ordinary income under applicable law. The economic investment loss is real before the sale; realizing it changes tax recognition, not the original market outcome. A tax benefit does not transform a bad investment result into profit. The investor must also think about market exposure after the sale and any possible repurchase restrictions.","Capital gains and losses must first be classified as short- and long-term and netted under Schedule D rules. A $5,000 short-term loss may offset gains subject to netting; if total capital losses exceed gains, the usual individual limit against other income is $3,000 per year, or $1,500 married filing separately, with eligible carryovers. If the only loss is $5,000 and no gains exist, claiming $5,000 against salary in that year would be wrong. The carryforward retains character under applicable federal rules.","Any 'savings' estimate must specify the offset rate. Offsetting $3,000 of ordinary taxable income at a stable 22% bracket could reduce regular federal tax by about $660, assuming no phaseouts. Offsetting $3,000 of long-term gains that would have been taxed at 15% has a simplified $450 tax impact. These effects depend on netting order and actual taxpayer facts. Do not apply a single 22% to every harvested loss."],"worked":["Taxable investment unrealized loss −$5,000 becomes realized through sale.","With no other capital gains, a single filer generally uses up to $3,000 loss against ordinary income in that year.","The remaining eligible $2,000 may carry forward rather than vanish.","At a stable 22% ordinary marginal rate, the $3,000 deduction would model ~$660 federal income-tax reduction before other factors."],"practice":[["Does harvesting a $5,000 investment loss create $5,000 of tax savings?","No. The investor already suffered an economic loss. The resulting tax effect depends on gain offsets, ordinary-income deduction limits, rates and eventual future consequences."],["Why might a loss carryforward not benefit the investor immediately?","The year's deductible amount against ordinary income is limited, and there may be insufficient capital gains to absorb the whole loss."]]},{"title":"Wash sales: the rule and the cross-account time window","body":["The federal wash-sale rule generally disallows a loss from selling stock or securities if substantially identical stock or securities are acquired within 30 days before or 30 days after the loss disposition. The rule therefore considers a 61-calendar-day period centered on the sale day, not merely the next month's trades. Substantially identical is a legal facts-and-circumstances concept; two ETFs tracking similar indexes should not automatically be declared safe or unsafe without examining their actual exposures and legal identity.","Wash-sale rules can capture purchases made through another broker or an account of a spouse in relevant circumstances, and acquisition in an IRA or Roth IRA can be particularly harmful because the loss may be disallowed without a normal recovery in replacement basis. A broker's 1099-B wash-sale box may only detect wash trades within its own reporting perimeter. The taxpayer remains responsible for identifying disallowed losses even when reporting is incomplete.","Automatic dividend reinvestment is a common trap: purchasing fractional shares of the same fund inside the window can create a partial wash sale. Other pitfalls include employer equity purchases, options and preplanned recurring buys. A disciplined harvesting workflow records sale date, all relevant accounts, share quantities, replacement asset characteristics and possible repurchase schedules before the order is sent."],"worked":["Sell tax lot at loss on November 10; inspect relevant purchases October 11 through December 10 inclusive as a teaching 61-day window.","An identical security bought November 20 can trigger a disallowance.","Buying replacement shares in a separately owned taxable brokerage account can still matter for the same taxpayer.","Check all accounts, including automatic reinvestment and spouse-related acquisitions where applicable."],"practice":[["Does waiting only 30 days after sale guarantee wash-sale compliance when the investor bought identical shares a week before sale?","No. The rule looks 30 days before as well as 30 days after the loss sale; earlier replacement purchases can cause disallowance."],["Why can a broker's Form 1099-B fail to capture every wash sale?","Its reporting may not see IRA acquisitions, spouse trades or trades at another broker; taxpayer-wide review is required."]]},{"title":"Disallowed losses and replacement basis mathematics","body":["A taxable-account wash sale often defers rather than permanently erases the economic loss. Suppose a lot with adjusted basis $10,000 is sold for $7,000, generating a $3,000 loss. If substantially identical replacement property is purchased within the rule's window for $7,500 and all of the original loss is disallowed, the replacement property's basis is generally increased by that $3,000 to $10,500. The original loss is not a current $3,000 tax deduction. Future disposition gain or loss is measured against the adjusted replacement basis, with applicable holding-period adjustments.","If the replacement property is later sold for $11,000 without another wash sale or special event, the simplified gain is $500, rather than $3,500 using original new purchase cost alone. The deferral moves tax consequences into the new lot. Partial replacement can require allocation rather than adding the entire disallowed loss to fewer shares. Work in units and tax lots, not just whole-account totals.","The IRA exception is important: when the replacement acquisition is in an IRA or Roth IRA, the disallowed loss generally cannot be restored by increasing that IRA's investment basis the way a regular taxable replacement lot is adjusted. The investor may permanently lose the current deduction. Education must strongly distinguish the standard taxable replacement case from retirement-account replacements."],"worked":["Old lot basis $10,000, sales proceeds $7,000 ⇒ apparent capital loss $3,000.","Identical replacement within the window costs $7,500 in taxable account; entire loss disallowed.","Adjusted replacement basis = $7,500+$3,000=$10,500.","Later sale for $11,000 without another wash sale: $500 gain before other adjustments."],"practice":[["Why would reporting a $3,000 realized loss immediately be incorrect in the example?","Acquisition of substantially identical securities during the wash window disallows the current loss; the replacement basis adjustment defers it in the taxable-account case."],["What is the danger of buying the replacement shares in a Roth IRA?","The loss may be disallowed without the ordinary taxable-account basis adjustment, potentially making the tax benefit permanently unavailable."]]},{"title":"Harvesting value depends on future tax and portfolio costs","body":["Deferring tax has a time value, but a deferral is not automatically permanent tax avoidance. If a $3,000 capital loss offsets taxable long-term gain at 15% today, the immediate federal regular tax reduction can be $450. If the replacement position later realizes a corresponding extra $3,000 taxable gain and the rate is again 15%, the future tax can be $450. The benefit is the value of timing and any legitimate rate difference, reduced by transaction costs and market tracking error. If the security is never disposed of during life, estate basis rules may alter the future, but this is not guaranteed and must be evaluated carefully.","A strategy might also increase basis by an amount that changes future loss or gain, or require replacement with a non-identical fund whose returns differ. Selling an equity fund and remaining in cash solely to avoid wash sale may create opportunity cost larger than the expected tax benefit. Conversely, staying fully exposed to a near-identical fund can raise wash-sale uncertainty. The portfolio decision must satisfy risk management goals even without its tax argument.","A scenario model should compare same-security hold, sell and remain liquid, and lawful alternative exposure with a documented tracking-risk assumption. Include commissions, spreads, realized gain rates, income-tax phaseouts, carryforward usage and both federal and state components. The 'headline tax saved' is a gross modeled input, not the decision's final net present value."],"worked":["Harvest loss $3,000 used against gain taxed at 15% ⇒ immediate tax effect $450.","If equivalent additional gain $3,000 is recognized in five years at 15%, future tax $450.","At a hypothetical 4% annual discount rate, five-year tax present value ≈ $450/(1.04^5) ≈ $369.87.","Timing benefit ≈ $80.13 before selling costs, tracking error and law/rate changes."],"practice":[["How can a fully deferred $450 tax saving be worth less than $450 in economic terms?","A corresponding future tax may still arise, so the benefit is primarily the present value of delaying payment and potentially changing rates, net of trading risk and costs."],["Why do transaction costs belong in a tax-loss harvest analysis?","Spreads, fees and replacement exposure differences can reduce or exceed the present value of expected tax savings."]]},{"title":"Connecticut taxes have a different starting point from federal taxable income","body":["Connecticut resident income-tax planning should begin with Connecticut adjusted gross income rather than simply importing federal taxable income or multiplying the federal liability by a fixed percentage. The state generally starts from federal AGI and applies Connecticut additions and subtractions; the state then uses its own personal exemption, tax computation and tax-credit/add-back structure. The federal standard deduction of $16,100 or $32,200 is not itself the Connecticut standard deduction, so using it twice misstates state liability.","Connecticut's 2026 withholding publication shows initial tax-rate schedules including a married-joint 2% band through $20,000 of the relevant calculation base, then a 4.5% band through $100,000, and higher bands thereafter. Crucially, the agency labels this an initial tax calculation; the final return can change through a personal credit, low-bracket phaseout add-back, and tax recapture. Using the initial schedule alone is an exercise in marginal mechanics, not a verified Form CT-1040 liability.","Consider a deliberately simplified MFJ state-tax calculation base of $90,000 after the applicable state-specific exemptions and adjustments. The initial state tax under that schedule is $400 plus 4.5% of the $70,000 above $20,000, or $3,550. Do not call $3,550 final state tax: apply the current official Connecticut tax calculation schedule and all relevant state credit and recapture rules before a real filing."],"worked":["Assumed Connecticut MFJ Table B calculation base $90,000.","Initial tax $400+0.045×($90,000−$20,000)=$3,550.","Possible personal credits, 2% phaseout add-backs and recapture are deliberately not modeled.","2026 official state filing schedules should be checked when issued; withholding tables alone are not final annual return instructions."],"practice":[["Why is federal taxable income not a safe replacement for Connecticut adjusted gross income?","Connecticut generally begins with federal AGI modified for state law, then applies state-specific exemptions, credits and tax computation rules rather than simply copying federal taxable income."],["What is the limitation of the $3,550 initial Table B calculation?","It excludes other required state calculations, such as personal credits, phaseout add-back, and recapture. It is not a final state tax amount."]]},{"title":"State differences can reverse a preferred federal investment choice","body":["Federal and state laws do not always grant the same preferences. A federal long-term capital gain potentially taxed at 0% can still enter a state's income-tax system. Connecticut generally taxes capital gains through its regular individual income-tax structure, subject to state-specific adjustments and credits. Similarly, qualifying US Treasury obligation interest can have a distinct state tax treatment from bank deposits and municipal bonds, while some other-state municipal interest may receive different Connecticut treatment. Eligibility needs instrument-by-instrument verification rather than a blanket 'all bonds are tax-free' claim.","Residence also matters. A person moving between Connecticut and another state in a tax year may have part-year residency, source income and allocation questions. A taxable brokerage sale on December 29 versus January 2 could differ in tax year or resident status, but manipulation of nominal address without genuine domicile change is not lawful planning. A student should learn to identify the controlling facts and seek official state guidance.","A rigorous comparative model contains two tax bases, two sets of rate rules, any valid state modifications, and a combined after-tax cash-flow result. It should explicitly show the case where a federal 0% preferential gain incurs a positive state tax, as well as how both marginal rates and state credits can interact. Do not automatically add two marginal brackets without checking both tax bases."],"worked":["Fictional eligible federal long-term gain $2,000 fits within a 0% federal capital-gain slice.","Federal regular tax on this isolated preferential gain can be $0 under stated stacking assumptions.","At an illustrative 4.5% Connecticut incremental rate with no credit/recapture effect, initial state tax effect ≈$90.","The 4.5% calculation is a limited sensitivity estimate, not a final CT-1040 result."],"practice":[["Why does a 0% federal long-term capital-gain result not always mean tax-free cash?","Federal preferential treatment does not force the state to exempt the gain. Connecticut's separate AGI and rate structure may impose state tax."],["Why should moving across a state border be analyzed using domicile and residency evidence?","Residency, source-of-income rules and actual facts determine state jurisdiction; choosing an address on paper alone does not lawfully decide tax liability."]]},{"title":"A documented decision policy is more useful than a tax trick","body":["Define a household tax-aware rebalancing policy before losses or taxable gains create emotional pressure. The policy should say which securities are eligible for loss review, the minimum economically meaningful unrealized loss, tax-lot selection protocols, the accounts checked for wash-sale conflicts, acceptable alternate holdings, and the deadline for checking official tax-year forms. It must also enforce investment-policy constraints: emergency cash and a home down payment are not speculative tax-harvesting reserves.","Tax-loss harvesting can be inappropriate when the sale undermines diversification, generates higher trading costs, creates wash-sale complexity or depends on future capital gains that may never exist. An investor in a very low tax bracket may derive little immediate benefit from offsetting a gain that would already be taxed at 0%, while potentially sacrificing a position that fits the long-term plan. Tax timing must be compared with investment risk, account access and future obligations.","A professional must never promise a strategy 'eliminates all taxes' without detailed lawful authority. Do not claim a trust, life insurance loan, or holding company automatically shelters personal income. Document assumptions, sources, warning triggers and referral to a CPA or qualified tax attorney where multiple states, estates, private entities, digital assets or large losses complicate the analysis."],"worked":["Write the investment lot, expected deductible loss, offset category and withholding effect.","Screen 30 days before and after across brokerages, spouse and retirement accounts.","Estimate present-value tax benefit net of spreads, market mismatch and possible future taxable sale.","Record official tax-year sources for federal and state computation and review with a qualified professional where necessary."],"practice":[["What two components make harvesting a finance decision rather than a tax-only decision?","The after-tax economic benefit must be evaluated together with portfolio exposure, liquidity, tracking error, fees and risk constraints."],["What is the core compliance lesson from trying to shelter investments in a trust?","Legal entity or title changes do not automatically eliminate federal or state income tax; applicable statutes, ownership and reporting rules must be established."]]},{"title":"The cumulative federal and Connecticut household case","body":["A household in Vernon, Connecticut wants to protect a future home down payment while managing a diversified taxable account. The fictional brokerage contains $24,000 market value and a tax lot with $10,000 adjusted basis now worth $7,000. The family may be able to realize $3,000 capital loss, but the same ETF was purchased automatically through dividend reinvestment two weeks earlier. Before analyzing rates, the student must identify possible wash-sale treatment and whether the loss is partially or fully disallowed.","The family also has a hypothetical $12,000 dedicated future home fund and a $6,000 emergency reserve. Neither belongs in the taxable investment pool for harvesting. The family's expected taxable income, any net gains already recognized, carryforward balance, credits and state residency must be recorded before calculating a tax effect. Model three alternatives: sell in spite of the wash sale, stop reinvestment and wait for a compliant window before any trade, or maintain exposure and make no tax trade this year.","The final investment committee memo must defend a decision under an unfavorable return scenario, a $2,500 unexpected repair and changes in tax rates. It must acknowledge that the most valuable outcome may be to avoid a complicated strategy and preserve housing cash. A fully correct worksheet can conclude that no trade is appropriate."],"worked":["Retirement/home obligations: $12,000 home plus $6,000 emergency cash, protected separately from brokerage.","Potential realized stock loss $10,000−$7,000=$3,000 before wash-sale analysis.","Identical automatic purchase two weeks before disposal can create a wash-sale conflict requiring share matching.","Demonstrate lawful options and explicitly calculate tax outcomes only after character, income, and disallowance are known."],"practice":[["Why is computing a $660 tax saving immediately from the $3,000 loss inappropriate?","A recent identical security purchase may disallow part or all of the loss, and actual offsets and applicable tax rates must first be determined."],["What is the decisive portfolio constraint in this household example?","The protected home and emergency funds cannot be treated as discretionary trading capital; investment actions must preserve essential liquidity."]]}],"sourceRefs":[["IRS Schedule D instructions: wash sales and netting","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i1040sd"],["IRS Publication 550: wash sales, holding periods and basis","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550"],["IRS Form 8949 instructions: adjustments and wash-sale code W","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i8949"],["IRS Topic 409 capital gains and losses","https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc409"],["Connecticut DRS IP 2026(7) official withholding calculation schedule","https://portal.ct.gov/-/media/drs/publications/pubsip/2026/ip-2026-7.pdf"],["Connecticut DRS tax information and estimated payments","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/resident-income-tax/tax-information"],["Connecticut DRS official calculators and tax calculation schedules","https://portal.ct.gov/DRS/DRS-Forms/Current-Year-Forms/Calculators-and-Tables"],["IRS 2026 federal tax brackets and capital-gain bands","https://www.irs.gov/irb/2025-45_IRB"]],"assessment":{"title":"Tax-loss, wash-sale and Connecticut committee case","prompt":"Prepare an investment-tax memorandum for a fictional Connecticut household with $12,000 dedicated home-closing cash, $6,000 emergency cash, and separately held taxable stocks. A lot with $10,000 adjusted basis trades at $7,000; an identical investment was automatically purchased 14 days earlier. Calculate the economic loss, explain whether it is immediately deductible and what additional tax-lot quantities are required. Analyze a distinct clean-sale example: old lot basis $10,000, proceeds $7,000, identical taxable replacement bought for $7,500 within the window; determine the fully disallowed loss, adjusted replacement basis and later gain if disposed of for $11,000 with no further wash sale. Calculate the initial 2026 Connecticut MFJ Table B tax at a stipulated $90,000 state calculation base, explain why it is not final tax, and evaluate timing value of deferring $450 taxes five years at 4%. Submit a one-page trading recommendation with sources.","rubric":["Loss recognition, capital-loss limits and tax character: 15 points","Wash-sale window, replacement matching and IRA risks: 25 points","Replacement basis and future disposition mathematics: 20 points","Connecticut computation and correct limitations: 20 points","Present-value analysis, protected liquidity and ethical reasoning: 20 points"],"answerKey":["Potential realized economic loss $3,000. Purchase 14 days earlier may cause wash sale; quantify actual overlapping replacement shares to determine disallowance.","Under fully matching taxable replacement example, $3,000 loss is disallowed currently; replacement basis = $10,500.","Later sale for $11,000 yields $500 gain assuming no further special events.","CT initial Table B: $400+4.5%×$70,000=$3,550, before personal credit, low-rate addback, recapture and other final adjustments.","PV of $450 due in five years at 4% is about $369.87; gross timing benefit approximately $80.13 before transaction and tracking costs."]}}],"capstone":{"title":"Module 11 · Connecticut household tax committee and portfolio decision","intro":"Cumulative 2026 teaching case. An eligible married couple files jointly, each under 65, with no dependents. They reside in Connecticut throughout the year. No other adjustments, credits, special capital-gain categories, NIIT or extra federal taxes; all gains/losses were recognized from valid, non-wash-sale transactions. The federal figures below use exact published bracket formulas rather than final 2026 tax-table rounding. The separate Connecticut initial-tax illustration deliberately assumes its calculation base and is NOT final CT-1040 tax.","scenario":["W-2 Box 1 joint wages $120,000; federal income-tax withholding $10,500.","Taxable bank interest $1,500; ordinary dividends $2,500, entirely qualified under holding-period rules.","Recognized net long-term capital gain before short-term netting $5,000; valid net short-term capital loss $3,000.","2026 joint standard deduction $32,200; ordinary brackets up to $24,800 at 10% and through $100,800 at 12%.","2026 joint zero-percent qualified-gain threshold $98,900 of combined taxable income.","Capital loss candidate: stock old basis $10,000, loss sale for $7,000; identical taxable replacement for $7,500 during wash window.","State teaching calculation: assume Connecticut's own adjusted calculation base is $90,000 under MFJ Table B, before credits/addbacks and recapture.","Protected cash: $6,000 emergency plus $12,000 home-closing goal; separate $25,000 diversified taxable investments.","Household has only $250/month conditional new savings; no unposted future income is counted as available cash."],"tasks":["Classify all revenue items and verify that qualified dividends are not double-counted.","Net the $3,000 short-term capital loss against the $5,000 long-term capital gain under simplified Schedule D treatment.","Compute 2026 gross income, AGI and ordinary taxable income before the preferential amount.","Calculate 2026 total taxable income after the joint standard deduction.","Compute ordinary federal tax using the first two 2026 joint ordinary brackets.","Calculate remaining 0% preferential capital-gain space and the tax on the qualified-dividend plus net long-term-gain amount.","Compute the illustrative federal regular income-tax liability and refund before any omitted features.","Recalculate federal tax if $2,000 additional ordinary wages are received, and explain the marginal rate.","Compare a $1,000 legitimate deduction versus a $1,000 nonrefundable tax credit with adequate liability.","Explain whether the proposed loss-sale/replacement transaction triggers a wash sale and what evidence is needed.","Under full taxable-account wash disallowance, compute the replacement basis and later gain if replacement sells for $11,000 without further adjustments.","Compute Connecticut's 2026 initial Table B tax on the stipulated $90,000 MFJ calculation base.","List CT final-return adjustments, credits, low-rate phaseout and recapture that prevent calling initial state tax a filed amount.","Value deferring $450 of tax five years at a hypothetical 4% annual discount rate before fees.","Design a near-term home/emergency liquidity and investment-policy constraint that bars use of restricted funds for harvesting.","Produce an 800–1,200-word committee memorandum with sources, alternatives, sensitivity table, limitations, and a defensible decision."],"answers":["Revenue classification: wage $120,000, interest $1,500, total qualified dividends $2,500 included once, net capital gain after netting $2,000.","Net recognized capital gain $5,000−$3,000=$2,000, assuming no special character or carried items.","Total income and AGI $120,000+$1,500+$2,500+$2,000=$126,000; no adjustments.","Taxable income $126,000−$32,200=$93,800.","Preferential amount $2,500 qualified dividends+$2,000 net long-term gain=$4,500. Ordinary taxable base=$93,800−$4,500=$89,300.","Ordinary regular federal tax = 10%×$24,800+12%×($89,300−$24,800)=$2,480+$7,740=$10,220.","Combined taxable $93,800 is below MFJ zero-percent $98,900 threshold; entire $4,500 eligible preferred amount uses 0% => $0 special federal regular tax under given assumptions.","Total modeled regular tax $10,220; withholding $10,500 => $280 refund, before tax table rounding and omitted items.","Extra $2,000 ordinary wages without altering other assumptions = $240 additional ordinary tax at 12% when the ordinary segment remains in the same bracket; re-evaluate preferences/credits as facts change.","A qualifying $1,000 deduction within the 12% ordinary band saves roughly $120; an eligible nonrefundable $1,000 credit with sufficient liability can save $1,000; tax and eligibility checks remain necessary.","Identical replacement acquisition within ±30 days of the loss disposition produces a wash sale for matching shares; review purchase quantities, accounts, dates and any partial matches.","Under full wash sale in a taxable brokerage, $3,000 current loss disallowed; $7,500 replacement cost+$3,000 basis adjustment=$10,500; sale for $11,000 => $500 gain (absent further adjustments).","Connecticut illustrative initial Table B for joint base $90,000: $400+4.5%×($90,000−$20,000)=$3,550.","Connecticut final return may require personal exemption, state AGI modifications, personal credits, 2% phaseout add-back, recapture and any other current return instructions; the initial figure is NOT final liability.","Tax deferred for five years: $450/(1.04^5)≈$369.87; simplified timing value ≈$80.13 before costs, risk and future tax-rate changes.","Keep $18,000 of essential obligations segregated and liquid; $250/month contributions only after bills and reserves are funded. Tax optimization cannot authorize spending protected funds.","Full credit requires accurately sourced 2026 federal and CT primary documents, reconciliation of all inputs, numerical sensitivity, written judgment and appropriate tax/legal referral triggers."],"rubric":["Federal return and classification, including capital netting (25%)","Progressive and preferential tax mathematics (20%)","Wash sale, loss carry and adjusted basis rules (20%)","Connecticut state comparison with limitations (15%)","Investment policy, liquidity, ethics and documented sources (20%)"],"sources":["https://www.irs.gov/irb/2025-45_IRB","https://www.irs.gov/instructions/i1040sd","https://www.irs.gov/publications/p550","https://www.irs.gov/taxtopics/tc559","https://portal.ct.gov/-/media/drs/publications/pubsip/2026/ip-2026-7.pdf","https://portal.ct.gov/drs/individuals/resident-income-tax/tax-information"]}};
